---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyCreditCardPayoff from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyCreditCardPayoff.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="credit-card-payoff-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyCreditCardPayoff client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How card interest is actually charged</h2>
    <p>
      A credit card APR is a nominal annual rate. Issuers divide it by twelve to get a
      <strong>monthly periodic rate</strong> and apply that to the balance you carry into the billing
      cycle. At 22.9% APR the monthly rate is 1.908%, so a 6,400 balance gets 122.13 of interest before
      any of your payment reduces the debt.
    </p>
    <p>
      This one fact explains how cards work. If your payment is below the interest charge, the balance
      grows, however regularly you pay. If it is just above, almost all of it goes to interest and the
      debt falls very slowly. If you enter a payment that cannot cover the interest, the calculator
      tells you and shows no payoff date.
    </p>

    <h2>The minimum payment is designed to be slow</h2>
    <p>
      A typical minimum is 2% of the balance with a small floor, calculated again each month. The
      percentage comes from a falling balance, so the payment also falls, and the gap between the
      minimum and the interest charge stays small for years.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Balance at 22.9% APR</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">First minimum (2%)</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">First month's interest</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Debt reduced</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>2,000</td><td style="text-align:right">40.00</td><td style="text-align:right">38.17</td><td style="text-align:right">1.83</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4,000</td><td style="text-align:right">80.00</td><td style="text-align:right">76.33</td><td style="text-align:right">3.67</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6,400</td><td style="text-align:right">128.00</td><td style="text-align:right">122.13</td><td style="text-align:right">5.87</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000</td><td style="text-align:right">200.00</td><td style="text-align:right">190.83</td><td style="text-align:right">9.17</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In the first month, less than 5% of a minimum payment reduces the debt. The 2% convention exists
      because it keeps the account open and revolving for as long as possible.
    </p>

    <h2>The fixed payment is the whole trick</h2>
    <p>
      The most effective change is to pay the same amount each month, not the amount the statement
      asks for. A fixed payment at today's minimum alone usually cuts a payoff of decades to a few
      years, because the gap between your payment and the falling interest charge grows each month.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two curves in the chart above show this. Both start from the same balance and the same rate;
      only the payment rule is different.
    </p>

    <h2>Working backwards from a deadline</h2>
    <p>
      If you know when the card must be clear (before a mortgage application, before a promotional rate
      ends, before the end of the tax year), switch the calculator to solve for the payment. It inverts
      the annuity formula:
    </p>
    <pre><code>A = B × i / (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code></pre>
    <p>
      It then checks the answer against the month-by-month simulation and adds a cent if monthly
      rounding would push you one month past the deadline. Statements round to the cent, and the
      closed-form answer does not.
    </p>

    <h2>Things that make the real number worse</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        New spending: the model assumes that you do not use the card again. Each purchase adds to the
        balance and, on most cards, removes the grace period until you clear the balance in full.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cash advances: usually a higher APR, no grace period, and a fee of 3–5%. Payments often go to
        the cheapest balance first, so an advance can accrue at 27% while your payment clears 19%
        purchase debt.
      </li>
      <li>
        Promotional rates that expire: a 0% balance transfer that reverts to 24.9% turns a good plan
        into a bad one overnight. Set the reversion date as your deadline and solve for the payment.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fees: annual fees, late fees and over-limit fees are not in this model.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A note on daily interest</h2>
    <p>
      Some issuers apply a daily periodic rate to the average daily balance, not a monthly rate to the
      statement balance. The difference is small, usually less than 1% of the total interest over a
      payoff plan, but it always costs you slightly more, because more days of compounding give more
      interest. Use the totals here as a close minimum, not an exact match to your statement.
    </p>

    <h2>Your balance stays in this tab</h2>
    <p>
      This page has no server, no analytics and no third-party requests. The plan, the schedule and the
      CSV, Excel and PDF exports come from JavaScript on your device, and everything works when you
      disconnect from the internet.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
